    {
      "subject": "Indonesian answer: merindukan",
      "conclusion": "Correct for 'to long for or miss someone'; KBBI defines it as strongly wanting or expecting to meet someone and as feeling love toward someone.",
      "material_risk": false,
      "evidence_refs": [
        "https://kbbi.kemdikbud.go.id/entri/merindukan"
      ]
    },
    {
      "subject": "Malaysian Malay answer: merindui",
      "conclusion": "Correct; PRPM defines merindui as having a strong desire to meet someone or obtain something that remains in one's thoughts or memories.",
      "material_risk": false,
      "evidence_refs": [
        "https://prpm.dbp.gov.my/Cari1?d=175768&keyword=rindu"
      ]
    },
    {
      "subject": "English cue and sense",
      "conclusion": "The narrowed cue 'to feel longing for someone absent' accurately disambiguates the intended emotional sense of 'to miss someone'.",
      "material_risk": false,
      "evidence_refs": [
        "https://kbbi.kemdikbud.go.id/entri/merindukan",
        "https://prpm.dbp.gov.my/Cari1?d=175768&keyword=rindu"
      ]
    },
    {
      "subject": "Final relation: similar",
      "conclusion": "Correct. The forms express closely corresponding meanings, despite different preferred suffixes.",
      "material_risk": false,
      "evidence_refs": [
        "https://kbbi.kemdikbud.go.id/entri/merindukan",
        "https://prpm.dbp.gov.my/Cari1?d=175768&keyword=rindu"
      ]
    },
    {
      "subject": "False-friend contrast",
      "conclusion": "A contrast should be added: Indonesian merindukan is the direct ordinary form for longing for a person, whereas Malaysian Malay normally uses merindui for that meaning; Malaysian merindukan is separately defined as causing or inducing longing, though it can have an additional 'long for' sense.",
